This forms part of clean-up routines (same of fixing bank spaces or unnecessary line breaks). In fact, `!` was not present in the Matrix code:   
https://github.com/xbmc/xbmc/blob/dc2adb742477bc95b0211be4ab4c2afdf3428b3c/xbmc/cores/VideoPlayer/VideoRenderers/OverlayRendererDX.cpp#L207

There is no reason why a clean-up should make the resulting code more difficult to interpret than the original code.

From a coding point of view

```c++
if (!condition)
{
  do_A(); 
}
else
{
  do_B();
}
```

is the same that:

```c++
if (condition)
{
  do_B(); 
}
else
{
  do_A();
}
```

I prefer second form because avoids unnecessary `!`

Yes probably this is the reason. I want to be sure that a copy of the object is not produced. I know these are common optimizations in c++ but they are still compiler dependent and these RVO and NRVO optimizations are not always guaranteed to occur.

And from a cosmetic point of view seems not looks bad as is in line with other calls:

```c++
  convert_rgba(o, USE_PREMULTIPLIED_ALPHA, min_x, max_x, min_y, max_y, rgba);

  glGenTextures(1, &m_texture);
  glBindTexture(GL_TEXTURE_2D, m_texture);

  glTexParameteri(GL_TEXTURE_2D, GL_TEXTURE_WRAP_S, GL_CLAMP);
  glTexParameteri(GL_TEXTURE_2D, GL_TEXTURE_WRAP_T, GL_CLAMP);
  glTexParameteri(GL_TEXTURE_2D, GL_TEXTURE_MAG_FILTER, GL_LINEAR);
  glTexParameteri(GL_TEXTURE_2D, GL_TEXTURE_MIN_FILTER, GL_LINEAR);

  LoadTexture(GL_TEXTURE_2D, max_x - min_x, max_y - min_y, o->width * 4, &m_u, &m_v, false,
              rgba.data() + min_x + min_y * o->width);

  glBindTexture(GL_TEXTURE_2D, 0);
```
